===== .agent-work/logs/issue-3-fix.log
- The server was started with plain nohup and its stdin was not controllable,
  so the workflow could not send "save-all flush" or "stop" before backing up.
  Only files already written by Minecraft were committed; in-memory chunks were
  lost when the runner was cancelled.
- Autosave ran only once every five minutes.
- A new run could check out the repository before the previous cancelled run
  finished pushing its final backup, then later overwrite the older changes
  with a stale world.
- sync-chunk-writes=true caused avoidable disk-write stalls on the runner, and
  pause-when-empty caused the server to resume lazily on the first join.

Changes:
- scripts/server-control.sh: FIFO-backed console control with start, command,
  save, stop and status commands.
- scripts/server-control.sh stop uses save-all flush followed by stop and
  escalates to SIGTERM/SIGKILL only if the server does not exit.
- .github/workflows/run.yml:
  * starts the server through server-control.sh
  * re-syncs from origin twice before starting to avoid stale-world overwrites
  * flushes and pushes the world every 90 seconds
  * traps SIGINT/SIGTERM during keep_server_alive to flush, commit and stop
  * always() final backup stops the server, clears interrupted git state,
    commits and retries the push
- server.properties: sync-chunk-writes=false, pause-when-empty-seconds=0
- README.md: documents the save/cancellation behavior.
- .agent-work/tests/test_server_control.sh: starts a disposable copy, checks
  console commands, save-all flush, graceful stop, and verifies a scoreboard
  value survives a full restart.

Validation run:
- bash -n and shellcheck pass for scripts/server-control.sh and the new test.
- actionlint passes for both workflows.
- .agent-work/tests/test_server_control.sh passed twice; the latest run also
  verified persistence across restart.

Latency:
- About 300ms RTT from Bangladesh to a US GitHub Actions runner is physical
  distance, not a save bug. The existing low-latency tunnel and TCP tuning are
  still the best available free path. A VPS in Singapore/India/Bangladesh is
  the only way to remove the distance.
